# Context

## Background
The description of the `sql` tool has a cap of 8,000 bytes
(`TestServerServesSQLTool`). The writer ID occurs two times in the
prompt. The test used the writer ID "dev1". So the test measured 56
bytes less than a release build, which has a 32-character device ID.

## Why This Way
- The recipes line said "filter session_id with =, never LIKE". It did
  not say how to search text. "search text with MATCH" closes that gap
  with 24 bytes and no new sentence. The prompt measures 7,907 bytes
  with a 32-character writer ID, 93 bytes below the cap.
- `ohya sql` passed a general remedy for `query_deadline`. The engine
  error now names the cause (254d0f4). A second remedy would repeat or
  contradict it. So the CLI passes an empty remedy and shows the engine
  hint.

## Gotchas
- The cap test uses a 32-character writer ID. Do not shorten it. A
  shorter ID hides bytes that a release build sends.
- The performance note in docs/performance.md states only what was
  measured: the engine without sinks. The other columns of that table
  were not measured again.

